  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1038 PM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nSome light shower activity is lingering this evening across\neastern Utah and is spreading into west-central Colorado as a\nremnant shortwave moves across the central and northern parts of\nthe CWA. Shower activity could linger overnight but only light \nrain or gusty winds would be the main concerns since the low \nlevels are still dry. The high pressure will remain overhead but\nget flattened a bit for Monday, still centered near the Four \nCorners. So for Monday, we will see moisture recycled under this\nhigh in westerly flow with another day of isolated to scattered\nshower and thunderstorm activity mainly over the higher terrain\nin the afternoon before drifting eastward into lower valleys\nlate in the day but collapsing as there is little in the way of\nupper level support to sustain any convection. The high gets\nnudged a bit east Tuesday but remains overhead so can anticipate\nmore of the same through Tuesday with gusty outflow winds the\nprimary concerns and low chance of wetting rains. \n\nBy Wednesday, a low pressure trough moves onto the west coast\nand nudges the high pressure ridge to the east in a favorable\nposition to advect deeper monsoonal moisture as the flow shifts\nto the south. The models have been honing in on the mid to late\nweek period for the best moisture availability and increasing\nchances and coverage of showers and storms with better chances\nof wetting rain. Several shortwaves will move through the\nsoutherly flow ahead of this trough as well Wednesday through\nFriday with the more robust activity expected Thursday.\nConvection stands a good chance to last into the overnight hours\nas well due to this added forcing and lift. Storms during this\nmid to late week timeframe will be capable of heavy rain, gusty\nwinds and hail with storm motion and speed playing a role with\nregards to heavy rain. For outdoor enthusiasts, please pay \nattention to the latest forecasts towards the end of the week. \nHigh temperatures will remain about 10 degrees above more normal\nAugust values, possibly dropping to below normal towards the \nend of the week thanks to increased cloud cover and precip.",
